  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Floods and landslides response in South Kivu &#x2013; DRC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The floods have caused significant damage to communities in the affected areas. To address this problem, Oxfam is proposing a WASH (water, sanitation and hygiene) intervention to address the lack of access to safe drinking water and sanitation facilities, which could lead to an increase in water -borne diseases.  The project will provide access to safe drinking water through the installation of emergency water treatment systems and the rehabilitation of existing water sources; sanitation facilities, i.e. latrines and hand -washing stations in health and school facilities; hygiene promotion activities, including training on proper hand -washing techniques and the importance of maintaining good hygiene practices. The project is an emergency project lasting 3 months (June to August 2023) and will provide access to drinking water through the installation of emergency water treatment systems and the rehabilitation of existing water sources, sanitation facilities, i.e. latrines and hand -washing stations in health and school establishments, hygiene promotion activities, including training in good hand -washing techniques and the importance of maintaining good hygiene practices for over 18,000 people affected by the floods in the Nyamukubi and Bushushu villages in Kalehe territory.  Protection activities will accompany all the planned activities, with the aim of minimising the risk of beneficiaries being exposed to further harm. Oxfam has the expertise to implement the project effectively and will work closely with other organisations working in the region to ensure that our efforts are complementary.</narrative>
  </description>
